# D1 continuation r8 — complete: the corpus run, the retention measurement, the bounds draft

## 1. The corpus run — 155/155 through the per-fixture gates

Every one of the 155 kernel-gate fixtures ran through the full r7 static gate — fresh recorded admission → persisted evidence/input/snapshot/both row closures → close → reopen → re-fetch → independent source hash + site declarations + binding reconstruction + C1 marker coverage → per-fixture declaration-mutation refusal. The honest enumeration in `collab/yang-clojure/1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r8.outcomes.edn` (worktree mirror; SHA-256 `7c2f9a6a…`): **155 `:pass`, 0 `:refused`, 0 `:not-run`**, each entry carrying its admission origin, storage delta, elapsed, row counts, sites, and `:mutation-refusal :site-declarations`. No fixture failed its gate — nothing to attribute this round; the refusal machinery is evidenced instead by the mutation checks (per-fixture declaration mutation on all 155; the five-class battery — `:inputs`, `:source-hash`, `:site-declarations`, `:bindings`, `:metadata` — refusing at exactly its own stage on real fixture data). r7's two known-good fixtures stayed green; cross-check: 20,805 total sites = r2's independent inventory.

Batched and interruption-safe by construction: batch 1 was killed at the 10-minute background limit (17 recorded) and its resume stopped (14 more) to fix a real scaling problem — per-fixture store close/reopen replays the whole content log (136 s/fixture at ~140 MB). The remaining 124 ran at one handle per batch invocation (cross-process reopen per batch — a stronger durability check). Recovery lost nothing: Σ per-fixture deltas = final store bytes exactly.

